  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 132 PM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nSaturday through Wednesday:\n\nA weak surface trough stemming from a broad upper low and \nassociated surface low crossing Ontario will sag through WI on \nSaturday. This will mainly bring a wind shift from southwest to \nwest, but the 850-700mb warm air advection and weak surface \nconvergence (enhanced by the lake breeze) may help trigger a few\nshowers or thunderstorms in the afternoon and evening. Saturday\nhigh temps will be in the mid 80s with dewpoints creeping back \ninto the 60s. It will feel warm and humid. \n\nSunday afternoon and night, and potentially into Monday, look \nto bring some more unsettled weather into the area. Overall, \nthere will be another broad, warm and humid ridge over the \ncenter of the country. This ridge should push into at least the \nsouthwest half of WI on Sunday and bump our highs to around 90 \nwith dewpoints pushing 70 (very warm and humid). Meanwhile, a \nmid level shortwave trough is expected to slide across the \nUS/Canadian border from MN to Lake Superior to Lake Ontario, \ncoincident with an 850mb baroclinic zone. It looks like \ningredients (moisture, instability, shear) will be in place over\nat least central WI Sunday afternoon/night for the potential \nfor a round of severe storms. The forcing would be the nose of \nthe low level jet and shortwave trough. There is more \nuncertainty for how far south the storms will get Sunday night.\n\nThe ridge will hang on over the central U.S. through much of \nthe next week. Several more shortwave troughs are expected to \ntrack across the Upper Great Lakes from Monday through \nWednesday, so additional rounds of storms (possibly severe) are \nlikely for central and southern WI. Each round will depend on \nhow the previous day's storms panned out. This looks to be an \nactive weather pattern setting up for WI. \n\nCronce",
